OK, I see that `asn1_get_octet_der` copies data from `der + len_len` to `str` when `*str_len > 0`. If `*str_len == 0`, nothing is copied, and `str` is left uninitialized.

But wait, in `x509_read_value`:
```c
if (len > 0) {
    result = asn1_read_value(c, root, tmp, &len);
    ...
    ret->size = (unsigned) len;
} else {
    ret->size = 0;
}

tmp[ret->size] = 0;
ret->data = tmp;
```

When `len > 0`, `asn1_read_value` is called with `tmp` and `len`. If the value is an empty string (length 0), `asn1_read_value` would set `len` to 0 (or 1 for the NUL terminator?). Let me check:

For `ASN1_ETYPE_BMP_STRING`, `asn1_get_octet_der` is called. If the DER data has a length of 0, then `*str_len = 0`, and `memcpy` is not called. `*ret_len = 0 + len_len`. Then `len` is set to `*str_len = 0`.

Back in `x509_read_value`:
```c
if (len > 0) {
    ...
    ret->size = (unsigned) len;
} else {
    ret->size = 0;
}

tmp[ret->size] = 0;
ret->data = tmp;
```

When `len == 0`, `ret->size = 0`, and `tmp[0] = 0`. `ret->data = tmp` (which is a 1-byte buffer with just a NUL).

Then in `decode_complex_string`:
```c
result = _gnutls_x509_read_value(tmpasn, tmpname, &td);
```

`td` is set to `{data = tmp, size = 0}`.

Then:
```c
if (etype != ASN1_ETYPE_INVALID) {
    result = make_printable_string(etype, &td, out);
    _gnutls_free_datum(&td);
    if (result < 0)
        return gnutls_assert_val(result);
}
```

In `make_printable_string`:
```c
if (input->data == NULL || input->size == 0) {
    out->data = gnutls_calloc(1, 1);
    if (out->data == NULL)
        return gnutls_assert_val(GNUTLS_E_MEMORY_ERROR);
    out->size = 0;
    return 0;
}
```

When `input->size == 0`, `out->data` is allocated with `gnutls_calloc(1, 1)` and `out->size = 0`. This is fine.

